<speak>
chapter 8. the i don’t need anyone lie.
<break time="600ms"/>
<break time="1.5s" />
<break time="600ms"/>
being the eldest daughter meant being responsible for everyone else, even your parents. you were the one making sure siblings did homework, settling arguments, keeping the family running. without you, the whole house might have fallen apart.
<break time="600ms"/>
that much responsibility so young matured you quickly, but it also carved something deep into you: the belief that you can, and must, do it all yourself. you learned never to ask your siblings for help (what could they do?), and never to lean on your parents (they had their own problems).
<break time="600ms"/>
the result? you became capable, reliable, indispensable. but also untouchable. even now, asking for help feels wrong, almost impossible. hyper-independence was your superpower as a child. as an adult, it’s the cage that keeps you exhausted and alone.
<break time="600ms"/>
this chapter is about unlearning the belief that needing others is weakness, and relearning that accepting help is part of being human.
<break time="600ms"/>
the help allergy test rate your reaction (0–10) to the following scenarios:
<break time="600ms"/>
someone offers to carry your groceries: …
<break time="600ms"/>
a friend insists on paying for lunch: …
<break time="600ms"/>
a partner says, “let me handle this task”: …
<break time="600ms"/>
a coworker offers to help with a project: …
<break time="600ms"/>
someone tells you, “you look tired, sit down”: …
<break time="600ms"/>
if you scored over 5 on most, you may have a “help allergy.”
<break time="600ms"/>
what did you notice in your body as you imagined these moments? did your chest tighten, jaw clench, stomach flip?
<break time="600ms"/>
that’s your body bracing against care.
<break time="600ms"/>
the disappointment museum think of three times you needed help and nobody showed up.
<break time="600ms"/>
age … : i needed …, but got ….
<break time="600ms"/>
how have these moments shaped the way you refuse help now?
<break time="600ms"/>
how would it feel today to let someone succeed in showing up for you?
<break time="600ms"/>
the help script when your brain screams “i’m fine” but you’re not, try these instead:
<break time="600ms"/>
“actually, yes, that would be helpful.”
<break time="600ms"/>
“i’m struggling with … today.”
<break time="600ms"/>
“thank you, i could use support with ….”
<break time="600ms"/>
“i’m learning to accept help, so yes.”
<break time="600ms"/>
“i don’t have to do this alone.”
<break time="600ms"/>
“i deserve rest too.”
<break time="600ms"/>
“it would mean a lot if you could handle this.”
<break time="600ms"/>
“i can’t carry this by myself anymore.”
<break time="600ms"/>
“yes, please . i need it more than i admit.”
<break time="600ms"/>
when was the last time you let someone help you without apologizing for it?
<break time="600ms"/>
reflection prompt what part of carrying it all alone hurts you the most?
<break time="600ms"/>
-challenge : for the next 24 hours: accept three small offers of help.
<break time="600ms"/>
remember this…
<break time="600ms"/>
hyper-independence was your shield, but it doesn’t have to be your identity. accepting help doesn’t make you weak, it makes you human. every time you let someone show up for you, you heal the part of you that once had no one. however, when you put down that shield, another survival strategy shows up right away: achievement.
<break time="600ms"/>
if you can’t rely on others, you prove yourself instead. you earn approval by excelling. you make yourself indispensable through performance. you tie your worth to what you can produce.
<break time="600ms"/>
this next chapter is about the trap of achievement and perfectionism: how the hustle that once kept you safe has now become the treadmill that never stops.
</speak>
